  "articleBody": "Interior partition framing might not carry the weight of your roof, but errors in layout and assembly quickly telegraph into finished surfaces. When you are ready to frame a new room or remodel an interior, avoiding these common missteps keeps finishes crisp and durable.\n\nCommon Mistakes to Avoid:\n- Inconsistent Stud Spacing: Wandering off layout creates irregular stud centers, leading to unsupported drywall edges and visible wall seams. Maintain crisp spacing across the run.\n- Tight Door Rough Openings: Failing to frame rough openings with adequate clearance prevents pre-hung door jambs from being shimmed plumb and square.\n- Missing Solid Backing: Forgetting blocking for heavy wall-hung cabinets, shelving, television mounts, or bathroom grab bars forces difficult retrofits after drywall is finished.\n- Crooked or Crowned Plates: Installing warped lumber for top or bottom plates produces bowed walls that make baseboards and trim pop loose.\n- Forgetting Utility Channels: Failing to plan penetrations for plumbing vents, water supply pipes, or wiring runs causes delays when specialty trades arrive.\n- Securing Walls Out of Plumb: Fastening framing permanently before checking both faces with a reliable level creates crooked door jambs and uneven tile surfaces.\n\nFor precise interior partition framing and complete finish work in the Denver region, reach out to Abraham Becerra at HH Remodeling LLC by calling 303-505-0866.",
